# Observations and Inferences

@body

![Slide image 3](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_003.jpg)

- *Observations*

- *Individuals in a population*
- *vary in their heritable*
- *characteristics.*

- *Organisms produce more*
- *offspring than the*
- *environment can support.*

- *Inferences*

- *Individuals that are well suited*
- *to their environment tend to leave more*
- *offspring than other individuals.*

- *Over time, favorable traits*
- *accumulate in the population.*

- *and*

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_004.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Population Evolution*

@body

- Note that individuals do not evolve; [*populations evolve over time*]{color=#8D281E}
- Natural selection can only increase or decrease heritable traits that vary in a population
  - the traits must already exist in the population, unless introduced through mutation

=== layout: one-panel

# *Natural Selection\: A Summary*

@body

- Individuals with certain heritable traits survive and reproduce at a higher rate than other individuals ([fitness]{color=#8D281E})
- Natural selection increases the match between organisms and their environment over time

=== layout: section

# Selection in a Laboratory Experiment

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Selection in a Lab Experiment*

@body

- Bacteria are great for studying selection, because they grow fast.
  - A study on the competition between two genotypes [A]{color=red} and [B]{color=blue}, under conditions in which [A]{color=red} is the superior competitor.
- We can measure [relative fitness, W]{color=#800080} as the competitive ability of of [B]{color=blue} when competing against [A]{color=red}.
  - [W]{color=#800080} &#8211; is called a relative fitness
    - Individual&#8217;s relative ability to survive and transmit genes to next generation

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Effect of selection for a favored allele (A) in a haploid (**_E. coli_**)*

@body

![Slide image 5](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_005.jpg)

- [A]{color=red} = 0.6 to .995
- [B]{color=blue} = 0.4 to 0.005
- [W]{color=purple} = 0.96 for B

=== layout: one-panel

@body

[Individual Fitness, w,]{color=blue} [Relative Fitness, W,]{color=#800080} and [Selection Coefficient]{color=#FF860D}

=== layout: one-panel

# *Individual Fitness*

@body

- [Individual Fitness, w]{color=blue}, is the ability to survive and reproduce.
- Each member of the population has its own [fitness value (w)]{color=blue}\:
  - 0 if it dies or fails to reproduce,
  - 1 if it survives and produces 1 offspring,
  - 2 if it survives and produces 2 offspring, etc.
- The [average fitness]{color=#00A933} of the population is calculated by averaging the fitness of individuals.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Average Fitness and Population Size*

@body

![Slide image 6](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_006.jpg)

- &#169; John Wiley & Sons, Inc.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Relative Fitness and Selection*

@body

- [*Relative fitness, W*]{color=#800080} is the contribution an individual makes to the gene pool of the next generation [relative]{color=red} to the contributions of other individuals
  - Selection favors certain genotypes by acting on the phenotypes of individuals
- [Relative fitness, W,]{color=#800080} is calculated by taking the superior genotype ([strain A]{color=red}) as standard fitness of 1.0
  - The [fitness]{color=#800080} of the inferior genotypes ([strain&#160;B]{color=blue}) is expressed as a deviation from 1.
  - [Strain B]{color=blue} produces only 0.96 offspring for a each offspring of a [strain A]{color=red}
  - [\^ 0.96 or 96% is the relative fitness \^]{color=#800080}

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Selection Coefficient*

@body

- Relative Fitness values\:
  - [Strain A]{color=red} has the standard fitness of 1.0
  - [Strain B]{color=blue} produces only 0.96 offspring for a each offspring of a [strain A]{color=red}
- [Selection coefficient (s)]{color=orange}\: The selective disadvantage of a disfavored genotype.
  - [s = 1.0 &#8211; 0.96 = 0.04 = 4%]{color=orange}
  - [The selective disadvantage]{color=#00A933} of [strain B]{color=blue} is 4% per generation
- The [selection coefficient]{color=orange} measures the intensity of natural selection acting on the genotypes in the population

=== layout: two-panels

# *Effect of selection for a favored allele (A) in a haploid (**_E. coli_**)*

@left

- [A]{color=red} = 0.6 to .995
- [B]{color=blue} = 0.4 to 0.005
- [*W*]{color=purple} = 0.96 for B
- [*s*]{color=orange} = 0.04

@right

