=== layout: section

# Selection in Diploid Organisms

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Selection in Diploid Organisms*

@body

- Dominance and recessiveness create additional complications.
  - Frequency of favored [dominant allele]{color=green} changes very slowly when the allele is [common]{color=green}.
  - Frequency of favored [recessive allele]{color=#706E0C} changes very slowly when the allele is [rare]{color=#706E0C}.
- Reason\: recessive alleles are found in heterozygotes, that are not exposed to selection.
  - remember Hardy-Weinberg and rare diseases

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme


@body

![Slide image 7](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_007.jpg)

- _Selection for a favored allele in a diploid depends upon whether the allele is dominant or recessive_
- _Favored recessive alleles take longer to take over_

=== layout: section

# True or False\: Saving lives of persons with rare recessive disorders (e.g. cystic fibrosis) will cause a deterioration of gene pool.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Rare Recessive Alleles and Selection*

@body

- Implication of widely held belief\: &#8220;_Saving lives of persons with rare recessive disorders will cause a deterioration of gene pool.&#8221;_
  - [NOT TRUE&#8230;]{color=red} reservoirs of recessive alleles is already in normal human beings who are heterozygotes.
- Selection for or against a recessive alleles is [*very inefficient*]{underline=true} when the recessive alleles is rare.

