=== layout: one-panel

# [*Natural Selection in the Forest Habitat*]{color=#1E6A39}

@body

- Assume that initially, p = 0.5 and q = 0.5 and that s1 = 0.1 and Hardy-Weinberg
- Genotype AA
  - relative fitness = 1, and the frequency of AA at fertilization is p^2^ = 0.25.
- Genotype Aa
  - relative fitness = 1, and the frequency of Aa at fertilization is 2pq = 0.50.
- [Genotype aa]{color=gray}
  - relative fitness = 1&#8211;0.1 = 0.9, and the frequency of [aa]{color=gray} at fertilization is q^2^ = 0.25

=== layout: one-panel

# [*2nd Generation (Forest Habitat)*]{color=#1E6A39}

@body

- In forming the next generation, each genotype will contribute gametes in proportion to its frequency and relative fitness.
- The relative contributions of the three genotypes will be
  - For AA, 0.25 &#215; 1.0 = 0.25
  - For Aa, 0.50 &#215; 1.0 = 0.50
  - [For aa, 0.25]{color=gray} &#215; [0.9 = 0.225]{color=gray}

=== layout: one-panel

# [*Normalize Proportions (Forest Habitat)*]{color=#1E6A39}

@body

- To obtain the proportional contributions of each genotype to the 2nd generation, divide their relative contributions by their sum
  - 0.25 + 0.50 + 0.225 = 0.975
- The proportional contributions to the 2nd generation are\:
  - For AA, 0.250 &#247; 0.975 = 0.256
  - For Aa, 0.500 &#247; 0.975 = 0.513
  - For aa, 0.225 &#247; 0.975 = 0.231
    - Note\: 0.256+0.513+0.231 = 1.0

=== layout: one-panel

# [*New Allele Frequency (Forest Habitat)*]{color=#1E6A39}

@body

- In the 2nd generation all of the alleles transmitted by [aa]{color=gray} homozygotes are [a]{color=gray}, and half the alleles transmitted by the A[a]{color=gray} heterozygotes are [a]{color=gray}.
- The frequency of a in the 2nd generation, symbolized by q' will be
  - [q' = 0.231  +  &#189; &#215; 0.513 = 0.487]{color=gray}
- This represents a loss of 2.6%
  - (0.5-0.487)/0.5 = 0.026

=== layout: one-panel

# [*Natural Selection in the Forest Habitat*]{color=#1E6A39}

@body

![Slide image 11](assets/lect15c-natural_selection/image_011.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# [*Natural Selection in the Field Habitat*]{color=#5EB91E}

@body

- Assume that initially,
  - p = 0.5 and q = 0.5 and that s2 = 0.1.
- Assume that the population mates randomly and that the genotype are present in Hardy-Weinberg frequencies at fertilization in each generation.

=== layout: one-panel

# [*Natural Selection in the Field Habitat*]{color=#5EB91E}

@body

- The initial genetic composition is\:
- For genotype AA,
  - relative fitness = 1&#8211;0.1 = 0.9, and the frequency of AA  at fertilization is p^2^=0.25.
- For genotype Aa,
  - relative fitness = 1&#8211;0.1 = 0.9, and the frequency of Aa at fertilization is 2pq=0.50.
- [For genotype aa,]{color=gray}
  - relative fitness = 1, and the frequency of [aa]{color=gray} at fertilization is q^2^=0.25

=== layout: one-panel

# [*New Allele Frequency (Field Habitat)*]{color=#5EB91E}

@body

- Using the same method as before, after one generation of selection in the field habitat, the frequency of _a_ will be
  - q'&#160;=&#160;0.513
- In the field habitat, the frequency of recessive _a_ alleles will increase in every generation until it equals 1
  - at a frequency of 1.0, it is said to be fixed in the population.

